  "draw": "[{\"factIndex\":1,\"question\":\"For how long a power outage does this guide prepare a household?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"a power outage lasting up to seventy-two hours\",\"sourceSentence\":\"This guide explains how to prepare an ordinary household to manage safely and comfortably through a power outage lasting up to seventy-two hours.\"},{\"factIndex\":2,\"question\":\"What climate is the guide written for, and what does it assume about a generator?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"a temperate climate and assumes no backup generator\",\"sourceSentence\":\"It is written for a temperate climate and assumes no backup generator.\"},{\"factIndex\":3,\"question\":\"In what order should you work the sections, and which matter most?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"Work through the sections in order; the earlier sections cover the preparations that matter most\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Work through the sections in order; the earlier sections cover the preparations that matter most.\"},{\"factIndex\":4,\"question\":\"How much water per person per day should you store, and how much in total for the 72 hours?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"four litres of water per person per day, which means twelve litres per person for the full seventy-two hours\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Store four litres of water per person per day, which means twelve litres per person for the full seventy-two hours.\"},{\"factIndex\":5,\"question\":\"How is the water allowance split between uses?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"Half of that allowance is for drinking, and the other half covers cooking and basic hygiene\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Half of that allowance is for drinking, and the other half covers cooking and basic hygiene.\"},{\"factIndex\":6,\"question\":\"How and where should stored water be kept, and how often replaced?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"food-grade containers, keep the containers out of direct sunlight, and replace stored water every six months\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Store the water in food-grade containers, keep the containers out of direct sunlight, and replace stored water every six months.\"},{\"factIndex\":7,\"question\":\"If a storm is forecast, what extra water step should you take, and what is that water for?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"also fill the bathtub; bathtub water is for flushing toilets and washing, never for drinking\",\"sourceSentence\":\"If a storm is forecast and you have advance warning, also fill the bathtub; bathtub water is for flushing toilets and washing, never for drinking.\"},{\"factIndex\":8,\"question\":\"How many days of non-perishable food should the dedicated shelf hold?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"sufficient for three days\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Keep a dedicated shelf of non-perishable food sufficient for three days.\"},{\"factIndex\":9,\"question\":\"Which foods are named as good choices?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, and shelf-stable milk\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Good choices are canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, and shelf-stable milk.\"},{\"factIndex\":10,\"question\":\"What kind of can opener must you own, and why?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"a manual can opener, because an electric one will be useless\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Choose foods that require no cooking, and make sure you own a manual can opener, because an electric one will be useless.\"},{\"factIndex\":11,\"question\":\"In what order should you eat food during the outage?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"the perishable food from the refrigerator first, then the food from the freezer, and only then open the emergency shelf\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Eat the perishable food from the refrigerator first, then the food from the freezer, and only then open the emergency shelf.\"},{\"factIndex\":12,\"question\":\"How long does an unopened refrigerator keep food safe?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"about four hours\",\"sourceSentence\":\"An unopened refrigerator keeps food safe for about four hours.\"},{\"factIndex\":13,\"question\":\"How long does a full versus a half-full freezer hold a safe temperature?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"A full freezer holds a safe temperature for about forty-eight hours, but a half-full freezer holds it for only about twenty-four hours\",\"sourceSentence\":\"A full freezer holds a safe temperature for about forty-eight hours, but a half-full freezer holds it for only about twenty-four hours, so it is worth keeping the freezer consolidated and full, even if some of the space is taken up by containers of frozen water.\"},{\"factIndex\":14,\"question\":\"What should you do with the refrigerator and freezer doors?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"Keep both doors closed as much as possible and tape a note to each door as a reminder\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Keep both doors closed as much as possible and tape a note to each door as a reminder.\"},{\"factIndex\":15,\"question\":\"When should perishable food be discarded?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"more than two hours above four degrees Celsius should be discarded; when in doubt, throw it out\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Any perishable food that has spent more than two hours above four degrees Celsius should be discarded; when in doubt, throw it out.\"},{\"factIndex\":16,\"question\":\"What primary light source is recommended, and what should you avoid?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps as your primary light source, and avoid candles entirely\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Use battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps as your primary light source, and avoid candles entirely, since candles are a leading cause of house fires during outages.\"},{\"factIndex\":17,\"question\":\"How many lights and spare batteries should you store?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"at least one light per person, plus two spare sets of batteries for each light\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Store at least one light per person, plus two spare sets of batteries for each light.\"},{\"factIndex\":18,\"question\":\"Where should headlamps and lanterns be placed?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"one headlamp beside each bed and one lantern in the kitchen, and place them where they can be found in the dark\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Keep one headlamp beside each bed and one lantern in the kitchen, and place them where they can be found in the dark.\"},{\"factIndex\":19,\"question\":\"Why must you never run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"each of these produces carbon monoxide, which is odourless and can be fatal\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Never run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window, because each of these produces carbon monoxide, which is odourless and can be fatal.\"},{\"factIndex\":20,\"question\":\"How many carbon monoxide alarms should you install, and where?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Install at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep.\"},{\"factIndex\":21,\"question\":\"In cold weather, which room strategy is advised, and what is safer than an improvised heater?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"one small room on a south-facing side of the home, close its door, and gather everyone there; layered clothing and blankets are safer than any improvised heater\",\"sourceSentence\":\"In cold weather, choose one small room on a south-facing side of the home, close its door, and gather everyone there; layered clothing and blankets are safer than any improvised heater.\"},{\"factIndex\":22,\"question\":\"At what indoor temperature should you relocate, and to where?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"below ten degrees Celsius and you cannot maintain warmth, relocate to a warming centre or a neighbour's home\",\"sourceSentence\":\"If the indoor temperature falls below ten degrees Celsius and you cannot maintain warmth, relocate to a warming centre or a neighbour's home.\"},{\"factIndex\":23,\"question\":\"What kind of radio should you keep, and what should you know beforehand?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"a battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts, and know the frequency of your local emergency station before the outage begins\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Keep a battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts, and know the frequency of your local emergency station before the outage begins.\"},{\"factIndex\":24,\"question\":\"How many times will a full 20,000 mAh power bank recharge a typical phone?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"a full modern power bank of twenty thousand milliamp-hours will recharge a typical phone roughly four times\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Charge every phone and power bank whenever a storm is forecast; a full modern power bank of twenty thousand milliamp-hours will recharge a typical phone roughly four times.\"},{\"factIndex\":25,\"question\":\"What phone mode and contact plan are advised, and why text rather than call?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"low-power mode at the start of the outage, and agree in advance on one out-of-area contact whom every member of the household will text, because text messages get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Put your phone in low-power mode at the start of the outage, and agree in advance on one out-of-area contact whom every member of the household will text, because text messages get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls.\"},{\"factIndex\":26,\"question\":\"What medical supplies should you keep, and for how many days of medication?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"a first-aid kit and a seven-day supply of every prescription medication, and rotate the medications so they stay in date\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Keep a first-aid kit and a seven-day supply of every prescription medication, and rotate the medications so they stay in date.\"},{\"factIndex\":27,\"question\":\"How long does refrigerated medication such as insulin remain usable at room temperature, and who should confirm it?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"up to twenty-eight days, but confirm the rule for your specific medication with a pharmacist\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally remains usable at room temperature for up to twenty-eight days, but confirm the rule for your specific medication with a pharmacist before an emergency happens.\"},{\"factIndex\":28,\"question\":\"Which phone numbers should you write down on paper?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"your pharmacy, your doctor, and the poison-control line on paper\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Write down the phone numbers of your pharmacy, your doctor, and the poison-control line on paper, because a dead phone is not a phone book.\"},{\"factIndex\":29,\"question\":\"How long should you wait before switching major appliances back on?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"Wait five minutes before switching major appliances back on\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Wait five minutes before switching major appliances back on, so the grid can stabilise.\"},{\"factIndex\":30,\"question\":\"When can freezer food be safely refrozen?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"if food still contains ice crystals, it can be safely refrozen\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Check the freezer: if food still contains ice crystals, it can be safely refrozen.\"},{\"factIndex\":31,\"question\":\"How soon should you restock what was used, and starting with what?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"within one week, starting with water and batteries\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Restock everything used during the outage within one week, starting with water and batteries.\"},{\"factIndex\":32,\"question\":\"What note should you write afterwards, and what should you fix before the next outage?\",\"expectedAnswer\":\"a one-page note about what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one of those gaps before the next outage\",\"sourceSentence\":\"Finally, write a one-page note about what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one of those gaps before the next outage arrives.\"}]",
  "transcript": "GAP (HOLD-APART ⿻ · xor) TRANSCRIPT — SALTED census mode\nInstance: examples/field-guide  Run: r5/r5.2  Proposal: restructure-merge-sections-inline\n\n== 1. NUMBERED FACT CENSUS (from artifact/GUIDE.md) ==\nRule: split the guide body into sentences on '.'/'!'/'?' in order; index every checkable-fact sentence (quantity, duration, temperature, item, instruction) from F1 upward.\nPopulation size required by this instance = 32. Counted = 32. MATCH.\nF1: This guide explains how to prepare an ordinary household to manage safely and comfortably through a power outage lasting up to seventy-two hours.\nF2: It is written for a temperate climate and assumes no backup generator.\nF3: Work through the sections in order; the earlier sections cover the preparations that matter most.\nF4: Store four litres of water per person per day, which means twelve litres per person for the full seventy-two hours.\nF5: Half of that allowance is for drinking, and the other half covers cooking and basic hygiene.\nF6: Store the water in food-grade containers, keep the containers out of direct sunlight, and replace stored water every six months.\nF7: If a storm is forecast and you have advance warning, also fill the bathtub; bathtub water is for flushing toilets and washing, never for drinking.\nF8: Keep a dedicated shelf of non-perishable food sufficient for three days.\nF9: Good choices are canned beans, canned fish, peanut butter, crackers, dried fruit, and shelf-stable milk.\nF10: Choose foods that require no cooking, and make sure you own a manual can opener, because an electric one will be useless.\nF11: Eat the perishable food from the refrigerator first, then the food from the freezer, and only then open the emergency shelf.\nF12: An unopened refrigerator keeps food safe for about four hours.\nF13: A full freezer holds a safe temperature for about forty-eight hours, but a half-full freezer holds it for only about twenty-four hours, so it is worth keeping the freezer consolidated and full, even if some of the space is taken up by containers of frozen water.\nF14: Keep both doors closed as much as possible and tape a note to each door as a reminder.\nF15: Any perishable food that has spent more than two hours above four degrees Celsius should be discarded; when in doubt, throw it out.\nF16: Use battery-powered LED lanterns or headlamps as your primary light source, and avoid candles entirely, since candles are a leading cause of house fires during outages.\nF17: Store at least one light per person, plus two spare sets of batteries for each light.\nF18: Keep one headlamp beside each bed and one lantern in the kitchen, and place them where they can be found in the dark.\nF19: Never run a camping stove, barbecue, or generator indoors, in a garage, or near a window, because each of these produces carbon monoxide, which is odourless and can be fatal.\nF20: Install at least one battery-powered carbon monoxide alarm on each floor where people sleep.\nF21: In cold weather, choose one small room on a south-facing side of the home, close its door, and gather everyone there; layered clothing and blankets are safer than any improvised heater.\nF22: If the indoor temperature falls below ten degrees Celsius and you cannot maintain warmth, relocate to a warming centre or a neighbour's home.\nF23: Keep a battery-powered or hand-crank radio for emergency broadcasts, and know the frequency of your local emergency station before the outage begins.\nF24: Charge every phone and power bank whenever a storm is forecast; a full modern power bank of twenty thousand milliamp-hours will recharge a typical phone roughly four times.\nF25: Put your phone in low-power mode at the start of the outage, and agree in advance on one out-of-area contact whom every member of the household will text, because text messages get through congested networks more reliably than voice calls.\nF26: Keep a first-aid kit and a seven-day supply of every prescription medication, and rotate the medications so they stay in date.\nF27: Refrigerated medication such as insulin generally remains usable at room temperature for up to twenty-eight days, but confirm the rule for your specific medication with a pharmacist before an emergency happens.\nF28: Write down the phone numbers of your pharmacy, your doctor, and the poison-control line on paper, because a dead phone is not a phone book.\nF29: Wait five minutes before switching major appliances back on, so the grid can stabilise.\nF30: Check the freezer: if food still contains ice crystals, it can be safely refrozen.\nF31: Restock everything used during the outage within one week, starting with water and batteries.\nF32: Finally, write a one-page note about what was missing or awkward, and fix at least one of those gaps before the next outage arrives.\n\n== 2. CANONICAL SERIALIZATION OF THE PROPOSAL ARTIFACT ==\nSerialization: JSON, recursive sorted keys, no whitespace, no trailing newline (arrays keep source order).\nPersisted bytes: proposal_canon.json (4729 bytes) in this scratch dir — the auditor re-hashes this exact file.\nHash command (exact): sha256sum proposal_canon.json\nDigest: 839170fae8b6a63b5f5044e976e08716c9b3130a9b8a7792e5506ae357d6af7f\nThis digest equals the GIVEN hProposal. MATCH — serialization did not drift.\n\n== 3. SEED DERIVATION (salted) ==\nRule: seed = sha256(hSource || hProposal || salt), the three GIVEN 64-hex strings concatenated as ASCII, no separators, no trailing newline.\nCommand (exact): printf '%s%s%s' <hSource> <hProposal> <salt> | sha256sum\n  hSource   = 011a4f3530017e0e6f394f1011efc9ee0233d96966cee000f0d5875d31d05b58\n  hProposal = 839170fae8b6a63b5f5044e976e08716c9b3130a9b8a7792e5506ae357d6af7f\n  salt      = e75b23a311d2292d76f423b3ee10048b701a86bd50117892b63e6576c4842792\nResult seed = 166281b1ebe2e42f0942a3ab727e172bd7d434a23c66f7c91caeb05eb8021e05\nThis equals the GIVEN seedHex. MATCH. (Seed and draw were engine-derived from a run salt the proposer never saw; not recomputed here beyond this verification.)\n\n== 4. DRAW ==\nMode = census. The engine drew every fact index without replacement, in order: [1..32].\nFor each drawn index, one comprehension question is written with the expected answer quoted verbatim from the ORIGINAL guide sentence (see draw field / section 3 of gap.json).\nDetection of a single dropped fact at census = 32/32 = 1.0.\n\n== VERDICT ==\nproposal_canon.json on disk hashes to hProposal; seed re-derives to seedHex; population = 32 as required; 32 questions drawn. Round is well-formed. The Gap judges nothing about the proposal's merits (per seat card)."
}